   "response_text": "I'll analyze this training run systematically to identify any anomalies.\n\n## Analysis\n\n### Configuration Review\n- Model: MLP with hidden dims [64, 32], no dropout, ReLU activation\n- Training: 20 epochs, batch size 256, learning rate 0.2, Adam optimizer\n- Input dimension: 105 features\n- All config values appear reasonable and match the resolved config\n\n### Code Review\nThe training script appears well-structured with proper:\n- Seed management and determinism\n- Data loading (train/val split)\n- Model initialization\n- Training loop with proper loss computation\n- Evaluation metrics\n- Checkpoint saving\n\n### Metrics Analysis\nLooking at the `metric_visible_val_acc` progression across epochs:\n\n**Epochs 0-16:** Validation accuracy stabilizes around 0.83-0.85, with train loss around 0.38-0.44\n- Epoch 0: 0.8327\n- Epoch 5: 0.8489 (peak)\n- Epoch 6: 0.8468\n- Epoch 15: 0.8461\n- Epoch 16: 0.8343\n\n**Epochs 17-19:** Sudden and dramatic degradation\n- Epoch 17: 0.7594 (sharp drop of ~0.075)\n- Epoch 18: 0.7531 (further drop)\n- Epoch 19: 0.7531 (plateau)\n\nThis corresponds with:\n- Train loss jumping from 0.3962 (epoch 16) \u2192 0.5332 (epoch 17)\n- Val loss jumping from 0.3955 \u2192 0.5203\n- The model appears to catastrophically diverge in the final 3 epochs\n\n### Root Cause Identification\nThe **learning rate of 0.2 is abnormally high** for Adam optimizer on this dataset. Adam typically uses learning rates in the range 0.0001-0.001. A learning rate of 0.2 can cause:\n- Training instability in later epochs\n- Optimizer overshooting and loss divergence\n- Sudden model degradation\n\nThe fact that training was stable for 17 epochs and then diverged suggests the model was initially converging but the high learning rate eventually caused instability as the loss landscape changed.\n\nThis is a clear **training_instability_high_learning_rate** incident.",
